{
  "dom_id": "step-q92276",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Which of the following Diophantine equations have solutions?\n\nI. $4x+12y=6$\n\nII. $2x+12y=4$\n\nIII. $-3x+5y=2$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d\\,|\\,c,$ where $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b).$  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each equation in turn.\n\n- Equation I has no solution. We have $a=4,$ $b=12,$ and $c=6.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(4,12)=4\n$$\n\nand $4\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,6,$ our equation has no solution.\n\n- Equation II has solutions. We have $a=2,$ $b=12,$ and $c=4.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(2,12)=2\n$$\n\nand $2\\,|\\,4,$ our equation has solutions.\n\n- Equation III has solutions. We have $a=-3,$ $b=5,$ and $c=2.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(-3,5)=1\n$$\n\nand $1\\,|\\,2,$ our equation has solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
